<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- The complete package budget. Adding anything here requires a recorded decision in AGENTS.md. -->

  <!--
    The Marten version, defined once and tested at both ends.

    MartenStudioMartenFloor is the version the shipped package depends on (`Marten >= floor` in its
    nuspec) and the one every build, test and pack uses unless told otherwise. Since 2026-11-03 the
    floors follow the latest releases (the user's policy of 2026-11-02: consumers keep up). 9.57.0
    fixes #5797, where compacting a stream that folds to null replaced its whole history with an
    empty snapshot, and brings JasperFx 2.98.0, Weasel 9.48.0 and Npgsql 9.0.4. Full record in
    AGENTS.md, *Package budget changes*, 2026-11-03.

    MartenStudioMartenLatest is the newest 9.x CI tests: the marten-latest workflow runs
    `dotnet fallout Test` with MARTEN_VERSION=latest, the Fallout parameter `marten-version`. It is an
    exact version, never a floating `9.*`: a red main caused by an upstream release would be nobody's
    pull request's fault. To bump it, change the value here, run both suites at it (the Fallout
    parameter set to `latest`, or `dotnet test` with -p:MartenStudioMartenVersion=<new>), and fix or
    record every difference in the same pull request.

    MartenStudioMartenVersion is what is actually restored. Leave it empty and it is the floor; set it
    with -p:MartenStudioMartenVersion=9.54.1, or through the Fallout parameter, which resolves `floor`
    and `latest` from the two properties above. Pack always packs at the floor and refuses anything
    else - the build's Pack target and a guard in src/MartenStudio/MartenStudio.csproj both say so -
    because a package packed at a newer Marten would silently raise every host's floor.

    (No double hyphen anywhere in an XML comment: it is not well-formed XML, and MSBuild answers a
    malformed Directory.Packages.props with NU1015 on every project rather than with a parse error.)
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Label="Marten version">
    <MartenStudioMartenFloor>9.57.0</MartenStudioMartenFloor>
    <MartenStudioMartenLatest>9.57.0</MartenStudioMartenLatest>
    <MartenStudioMartenVersion Condition="'$(MartenStudioMartenVersion)' == ''">$(MartenStudioMartenFloor)</MartenStudioMartenVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Library (src/MartenStudio)">
    <!-- The only PackageReference the shipped RCL makes. Everything else it needs is in the
         Microsoft.AspNetCore.App shared framework (FrameworkReference), which is what keeps the
         package from pinning an ASP.NET Core patch on its host. Floor, not exact pin: a consumer
         on a newer Marten 9.x must not be forced down. Npgsql arrives transitively via Weasel.Postgresql.

         The floor is 9.57.0 (2026-11-03): the newest release, which removes the compaction hazard
         of #5797 instead of guarding it. Older bisections (9.31.0 for the code, 9.39.0 for the
         advisories) are history in AGENTS.md, *Package budget changes*. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Marten" Version="$(MartenStudioMartenVersion)" />
    <!--
      blazor.web.js. As of .NET 10 it is not in the shared framework and not embedded in any ASP.NET
      Core assembly: it lives in this pack, which the SDK resolves only for a project whose
      `RequiresAspNetWebAssets` is true - and the Web SDK defaults that to true only for a project with
      `.razor` Content items of its own. A host that adds Marten Studio and writes no Blazor components
      has none, so GET /_framework/blazor.web.js answered 404 and the studio prerendered and never became
      interactive. The package cannot set the property on the consumer's behalf (NuGet excludes
      package-provided .props from restore evaluation), so the reference is how the script reaches the
      host: its buildTransitive targets define the asset IN THE CONSUMER, at the consumer's own resolved
      version, which is also what keeps the studio from shipping a frozen copy of a Microsoft script.
      No lib/, no ref/, no runtime assembly - three .js files and an MSBuild target - so it pins no
      ASP.NET Core patch on the host. Floor, not exact pin, like Marten. Recorded in AGENTS.md,
      *Package budget changes*, 2026-09-14.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App.Internal.Assets" Version="10.0.12" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Sample and integration tests">
    <!-- Zero-config `dotnet run`: with no connection string configured the sample starts its own
         throwaway Postgres. Also the container the integration tests use. Recorded in AGENTS.md;
         the sample is never packed. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Testcontainers.PostgreSql" Version="4.15.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Testcontainers.Kafka" Version="4.15.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Test - both projects">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="18.10.0" />
    <!--
      Held at 3.x deliberately: xunit.v3 4.0.0 drops VSTest-mode support, which Fallout's
      ITest depends on (it passes `logger trx;LogFileName=` and parses the TeamTest/2010 TRX).
      See AGENTS.md, *Package budget changes*, for the revisit trigger.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3" Version="3.2.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.5" />
    <!--
      Fallout's ITest probes each test project for this reference and only then passes
      `logger GitHubActions`. Without it a red CI run is a TRX file and nothing else.

      HELD AT 2.4.1, the last VSTest-only release. 3.x is a Microsoft.Testing.Platform logger: it is
      compiled against `Microsoft.Testing.Platform` 2.0.0, while `xunit.v3` 3.2.2 resolves 1.9.1, and
      the two are not assembly-version compatible - both test projects failed to compile with
      CS1705 ("Microsoft.Testing.Platform ... uses ... which has a higher version than referenced
      assembly"). 2.4.1 depends only on `Microsoft.TestPlatform.ObjectModel`, which is exactly the
      VSTest bridge Fallout's ITest drives (`logger trx` + `logger GitHubActions`), so it
      is the release that matches the rest of the budget rather than the newest one. Pinning
      `Microsoft.Testing.Platform` forward instead would fight xunit.v3's own resolution and is not
      done. Revisit when xunit.v3 and Fallout's ITest both speak Microsoft.Testing.Platform v2.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="GitHubActionsTestLogger" Version="2.4.1" />
    <!-- MIT fork of FluentAssertions. One assertion library, no mocking library: the data seam is
         our own set of interfaces and hand-written fakes are smaller and more auditable than a
         mocking framework's call-matching DSL. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="AwesomeAssertions" Version="9.6.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Test - MartenStudio.Tests (no Docker)">
    <PackageVersion Include="bunit" Version="2.13.3" />
    <!--
      PINNED AT 32.0.0 ON PURPOSE. Verify.XunitV3 33.x depends on xunit.v3.extensibility.core
      4.0.1; 32.0.0 is the last release that depends on 3.2.2, which is where xunit.v3 is held.
      Moving this forward silently forces xunit.v3 4.x and breaks Fallout's TRX reporting.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Verify.XunitV3" Version="32.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="PublicApiGenerator" Version="11.5.4" />
    <!-- `builder.WebHost.UseTestServer()` for the endpoint-metadata and startup-guard tests.
         TestHost rather than Mvc.Testing: these tests never need WebApplicationFactory. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost" Version="10.0.12" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Test - MartenStudio.Integration.Tests (needs Docker)">
    <!-- WebApplicationFactory over the real sample host. 11.0.0 is rc; do not take it. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" Version="10.0.12" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Playwright" Version="1.62.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The Npgsql version of the PostgresStudio-only projects, defined once and tested at both ends, like
    Marten's above.

    PostgresStudioNpgsqlFloor is what the PostgresStudio package depends on (`Npgsql >= floor` in its
    nuspec). It must never be above the Npgsql the Marten floor resolves, or PostgresStudio would raise
    every MartenStudio host's Npgsql through the ProjectReference (NpgsqlVersionTests reads
    src/MartenStudio's restore to check). 9.0.4 is that Npgsql: the one Marten 9.57.0 itself
    references. Since 2026-11-03 nothing older is supported (AGENTS.md, Package budget changes);
    the 8.0.3 floor of PS-7 is history.

    PostgresStudioNpgsqlLatest is the newest Npgsql CI tests PostgresStudio at: the marten-latest
    workflow sets NPGSQL_VERSION=latest, the Fallout parameter `npgsql-version`. It applies ONLY to the
    second pass over postgres-studio.slnf (build/Build.Npgsql.cs), which holds the projects that set
    PostgresStudioPinsNpgsql. Passed to the whole solution it would raise MartenStudio and its tests to
    that Npgsql through the ProjectReference, with no diagnostic, and MartenStudio on Npgsql 10 is not
    a tested combination.

    PostgresStudioNpgsqlVersion is what those projects restore. Empty means the floor. Pack packs
    PostgresStudio at the floor only: the build's Pack target and src/PostgresStudio/PostgresStudio.csproj
    both refuse anything else.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Label="PostgresStudio Npgsql version">
    <PostgresStudioNpgsqlFloor>9.0.4</PostgresStudioNpgsqlFloor>
    <PostgresStudioNpgsqlLatest>10.0.3</PostgresStudioNpgsqlLatest>
    <PostgresStudioNpgsqlVersion Condition="'$(PostgresStudioNpgsqlVersion)' == ''">$(PostgresStudioNpgsqlFloor)</PostgresStudioNpgsqlVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="PostgresStudio-only projects (they set PostgresStudioPinsNpgsql)">
    <PackageVersion Include="Npgsql" Version="$(PostgresStudioNpgsqlVersion)" Condition="'$(PostgresStudioPinsNpgsql)' == 'true'" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The Confluent.Kafka version of the KStudio projects, defined once and tested at both ends, like Marten's above.
    KStudioConfluentFloor is what KStudio.Core depends on: 2.14.0, the version WolverineFx.Kafka 6.61 pins, so a
    Wolverine host is never raised (AGENTS.md, Package budget changes, 2026-11-09). KStudioConfluentLatest is the newest
    release the suites are also run at. KStudioConfluentVersion is what is restored; empty means the floor. Only the
    KStudio projects reference Confluent.Kafka, so the property may be passed to the whole solution.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Label="KStudio Confluent.Kafka version">
    <KStudioConfluentFloor>2.14.0</KStudioConfluentFloor>
    <KStudioConfluentLatest>2.16.0</KStudioConfluentLatest>
    <KStudioConfluentVersion Condition="'$(KStudioConfluentVersion)' == ''">$(KStudioConfluentFloor)</KStudioConfluentVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="KStudio (src/KStudio.Core)">
    <PackageVersion Include="Confluent.Kafka" Version="$(KStudioConfluentVersion)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Wolverine's Kafka transport (KStudio M4, WOLV; AGENTS.md D43; Package budget changes, 2026-11-1x entry "WolverineFx.Kafka 6.61.0").
    Referenced by src/KStudio.Wolverine, tests/KStudio.Integration.Tests and samples/KStudio.Sample (tests/KStudio.Wolverine.Tests takes it through its reference to the adapter). 6.61.0
    pins Confluent.Kafka 2.14.0, KStudio.Core's floor, so a Wolverine host is never raised by KStudio. It brings JasperFx 2.99.0, above Marten
    9.57.0's 2.98.0: a project that holds both resolves 2.99.0, as every Marten-plus-Wolverine host does. Unconditional: none of the shipped
    studio packages or KStudio / KStudio.Core reference it (KStudioWolverineFenceTests).
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="KStudio.Wolverine (src/KStudio.Wolverine)">
    <PackageVersion Include="WolverineFx.Kafka" Version="6.61.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Wolverine's runtime code generation, for samples/KStudio.Sample only (KStudio M4, SAMPLE-M4; Package budget changes, 2026-11-12 entry
    "WolverineFx.RuntimeCompilation 6.61.0"). Wolverine 6.61 no longer ships its Roslyn compiler, and a host with a handler needs this package
    (or pre-generated code) to start. Version matches WolverineFx 6.61.0. No shipped project and no test project references it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="KStudio sample (samples/KStudio.Sample)">
    <PackageVersion Include="WolverineFx.RuntimeCompilation" Version="6.61.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The Microsoft.Extensions.* packages of src/PostgresStudio.Diagnostics, which has no FrameworkReference to
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.App and so needs the abstractions it uses as packages (AGENTS.md, Package budget
    changes, 2026-11-01). They are floors, not exact pins.

    Why 10.0.0 and not 8.0.x: Npgsql 10.0.3's net10.0 dependency group asks for
    Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions 10.0.0. Measured with the rows at 8.0.0 and
    PostgresStudioNpgsqlVersion=10.0.3: restore raises no NU code and resolves Logging.Abstractions 8.0.0 under it,
    the central pin silently winning, and a floor that lets the resolved set sit below what the driver asks for is not
    a floor. The library is net10.0 only (D1), so nothing older than the first net10.0 release is a host this package
    could meet.

    Why conditional: an unconditional row would pin, through CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled, the
    Microsoft.Extensions.* that Marten, JasperFx and Weasel bring in MartenStudio and its tests. Only a project
    that sets PostgresStudioDiagnosticsPinsExtensions (the Diagnostics project and the worker sample) sees the rows.

    Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting is the worker sample's only addition (0.11-D, Package budget changes 2026-11-01): the
    generic host a worker runs in, which no shipped package references.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="PostgresStudio.Diagnostics and KStudio.Core (they set PostgresStudioDiagnosticsPinsExtensions or KStudioCorePinsExtensions)" Condition="'$(PostgresStudioDiagnosticsPinsExtensions)' == 'true' or '$(KStudioCorePinsExtensions)' == 'true'">
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" Version="10.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
